                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "This game has something special that I've struggled to find elsewhere: personality.\n\nIts customization is, hands down, unrivaled. Being able to mix and match armor while keeping stats separate from appearance gives players an incredible freedom of expression. Combined with the charm of Artix Entertainment's games, it's cute, funny, and occasionally serious. It is truly iconic! There's always something to do, from exploring beautiful maps and completing quests to tackling dungeons, learning classes, and playing minigames. The community is wonderful, too, making this a game I'd happily recommend to new and casual players, especially since it's free.\n\nBut despite everything I love about this game, its reward systems increasingly undermine what makes it special.\n\nThe endgame loot system creates a cycle of FOMO that encourages players to rush through content rather than enjoy it. When a new boss and its rare rewards arrive, players flock to it, leaving older bosses abandoned. Since these fights typically require a group, obtaining older rewards can become unnecessarily difficult simply because the community has moved on. New players are then pressured to rush through storylines to reach the latest content before its popularity fades, potentially missing the very world and narrative the developers worked hard to create.\n\nThen there's the grind itself. Some of the best weapons are locked behind difficult bosses with extremely low drop rates. You might get lucky in three fights, or spend over a hundred trying for the same item. Rare rewards are fine, but without crafting, influencing drops, or some form of guaranteed progress, players can sink hours into an encounter with nothing to show for it.\n\nThis pushes players toward relentless optimization, sometimes even glitches, rather than enjoying the fights as intended. When efficiency becomes more important than cooperation, frustration follows, and even a generally kind community can become bitter toward players who don't meet the meta's expectations.\n\nThese systems might be commonplace in MMOs, but that's precisely what makes them so disappointing here. A game built around creativity, charm, and freedom ends up encouraging urgency, repetition, and conformity. Instead of letting players enjoy the game on their own terms, its reward structure pressures them to chase whatever is currently relevant as efficiently as possible.\n\nI don't think rare loot or challenging bosses need to disappear. I'd simply like systems that respect players' time: meaningful progress toward desired rewards, better ways to obtain older gear, and less pressure to rush through content before the community moves on.\n\nI genuinely love this game. Its customization, world, humor, and community are exceptional. I just wish its progression systems supported those qualities instead of working against them.\n\nI love this game: I've bought the Guardian upgrade, and have sunk countless hours into it. I just don't find it fun nor respectful anymore. Perhaps they build a single player \"DragonFable 3D\" that builds upon the framework laid by AdventureQuest 3D but with the independence of DragonFable - I'd happily pay money for that game!\n\n(Review created pre-'Loot 2.0' changes)",
                        "timestamp_created": 1792033021,
